WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.611 --> 00:00:02.699
- A nest is a place where you have

00:00:02.699 --> 00:00:05.427
a sense of protection and strength.

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:09.112
It's like an earth temple
or a nature temple.

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:11.029
The wood is following a pattern,

00:00:11.029 --> 00:00:13.612
but not one branch is the same.

00:00:14.864 --> 00:00:18.773
As a nest builder, I'm
following those patterns.

00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:20.747
Most of the work is trying to bring out

00:00:20.747 --> 00:00:24.164
the best of those shapes with each other.

00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:32.638
(elegant violin music)

00:00:32.638 --> 00:00:35.518
I'm Jayson Fann, and I'm a nest builder.

00:00:35.518 --> 00:00:37.947
I started building nests as a child.

00:00:37.947 --> 00:00:40.747
The nest building was
really an intuitive process

00:00:40.747 --> 00:00:45.046
of just gathering branches
and building forts originally.

00:00:45.046 --> 00:00:49.213
And the nest grew and got
bigger and more elaborate.

00:00:50.078 --> 00:00:52.070
Now some of the nests are as big

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.153
as a hundred thousand pounds of wood.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:00:59.190
The hardest part of building

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.087
the nest is really gathering all the wood.

00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:05.588
I use a chainsaw to cut wood.

00:01:05.588 --> 00:01:09.135
I use a machete to cut
off all the excess leaves

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.199
and from there, I really assess,

00:01:11.199 --> 00:01:12.644
you know, what kind of shapes I have,

00:01:12.644 --> 00:01:15.026
what kind of material I have to work with.

00:01:15.026 --> 00:01:18.455
The construction process
of the nest first is creating

00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:21.076
the foundation and the infrastructure.

00:01:21.076 --> 00:01:24.606
And then I follow those
larger sculptural shapes

00:01:24.606 --> 00:01:27.937
that I bolt together with smaller material

00:01:27.937 --> 00:01:30.572
that I can weave in and around.

00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:32.206
I use every part of the tree,

00:01:32.206 --> 00:01:33.945
the trunk of the tree, all the way down

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:37.132
to the very smallest twig
and branch of the tree.

00:01:37.132 --> 00:01:39.262
I've gotten to where I'm
used to building them

00:01:39.262 --> 00:01:41.285
on flatbed trailers
and moving them around,

00:01:41.285 --> 00:01:45.036
and in many cases, I'm transporting
them across the country.

00:01:45.036 --> 00:01:47.527
Because the nests are quite heavy,

00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:52.228
I use cranes and forklifts and
different kinds of equipment.

00:01:52.228 --> 00:01:53.333
This is all eucalyptus.

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:56.097
There's over 75 different species

00:01:56.097 --> 00:01:58.205
of eucalyptus on this property.

00:01:58.205 --> 00:02:00.565
I've built around 50
nests all over the country,

00:02:00.565 --> 00:02:02.995
and now primarily, I build the nests

00:02:02.995 --> 00:02:06.606
with kids within an educational setting.

00:02:06.606 --> 00:02:09.863
As an educator, like, it's
really important to work

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:13.451
with kids and help them
realize that they have

00:02:13.451 --> 00:02:16.567
the ability to shape and
create an environment.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.391
I'm gonna show you how we can integrate

00:02:18.391 --> 00:02:21.059
those branches into the spiral.

00:02:21.059 --> 00:02:24.049
I love getting kids out into nature,

00:02:24.049 --> 00:02:25.649
interacting with nature.

00:02:25.649 --> 00:02:27.279
And the nest is actually very much

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:30.377
a collaboration with nature.

00:02:30.377 --> 00:02:32.412
I know I've done a good
job when it looks like

00:02:32.412 --> 00:02:33.677
the nest is something that could

00:02:33.677 --> 00:02:36.677
almost have grown out of the ground.

